(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-cp-exam-1-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"Clin Path I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why do cancers spreading through the portal vein and the inferior vena cava reach the liver and the lungs?","choices":["Because tumor cells preferentially enter arterial rather than venous blood","Because lymphatic drainage from most organs terminates in the liver and lungs","Because those are the organs each vein drains into","Because liver and lung tissue are uniquely receptive to any tumor cell"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — hematogenous spread is typically venous, so fragments in the portal vein reach the liver and those in the inferior vena cava reach the lungs. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why can a neutrophil kill only a limited number of pathogens before dying?","choices":["It converts into a macrophage after a single encounter","It cannot renew its lysosomes, so dies after phagocytosing a few","It lacks the enzymes needed to digest the bacterial cell wall it engulfs","It must first be opsonized before it can engulf anything"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils are not able to renew their lysosomes, so they die after having phagocytosed a few pathogens. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is inflammation?","choices":["The death of tissue through coagulative or liquefactive pathways","The reaction of vascularized living tissue to injury","The proliferation of fibroblasts following any cellular insult","The activation of the adaptive immune system by a foreign antigen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Inflammation is defined as the reaction of vascularized living tissue to injury, so living tissue with a blood supply is part of the definition. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What gives the spinous layer its alternative name of prickle cell layer?","choices":["Dead flattened keratinocytes packed into scales","Keratinocytes connected by intercellular connections called desmosomes, which form a spiny appearance","Keratohyalin granules that give the cells a rough and irregular outline when viewed on microscopy","Melanin granules transferred from melanocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The 8 to 10 keratinocyte layers of the stratum spinosum are joined by desmosomes, which give the spiny look behind the name prickle cell layer. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Macular degeneration is the leading cause of new-onset blindness in which group?","choices":["Adults over seventy-five in the United States","Adults aged twenty to seventy-four in the United States","Children and adolescents","Adults of any age worldwide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — macular degeneration is the leading cause of new-onset blindness in United States adults over seventy-five, while diabetic retinopathy leads in younger adults. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the functional consequence of presbyopia?","choices":["Loss of peripheral vision","Loss of color discrimination","Inability to focus on near objects, so reading glasses are needed","Inability to focus on distant objects, so distance vision blurs first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the sclerotic lens and ciliary muscle can no longer accommodate, so near objects cannot be focused and readers or bifocals are needed. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which description of squamous cell carcinoma is accurate?","choices":["Autosomal dominant inheritance with variable penetrance and radial growth","Mutation in the tp53 tumor suppressor gene, with keratin pearls and epithelial pearls as pathognomonic features","Overactivation of the Hedgehog pathway, with pearly papules and telangiectasias","Activating mutation of fibroblast growth factor receptor-3, with a stuck-on waxy and scaly appearance on the skin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma involves mutation of the tp53 tumor suppressor gene in keratinocytes, and keratin and epithelial pearls are pathognomonic of it. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which description of Henoch-Schonlein purpura is accurate?","choices":["The skin alone, with no systemic involvement whatsoever, at any age at all","Skin, joints, kidneys and intestines, commonly in children aged 3 to 10 years","Skin and lungs only, commonly in older adults","Skin and liver, commonly in neonates"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — an immunoglobulin A vasculitis, which is why it is not confined to the skin. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which pairing of response and mediator describes the basophil's chief role?","choices":["Antibody production, through immunoglobulin G","Cell-mediated immunity, through T cell receptors","Allergic and antigen response, through histamine","Parasitic defense, through major basic protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Basophils drive allergic and antigen response via histamine, which causes inflammation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which history most separates labyrinthitis from Meniere disease?","choices":["A recent upper respiratory infection","The presence of sensorineural hearing loss","Unilateral rather than bilateral symptoms","The presence of tinnitus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Labyrinthitis is caused by viral infection, typically a recent viral upper respiratory infection, whereas Meniere disease arises from defective endolymph resorption causing hydrops. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What happens to proliferation once a cell has committed to differentiation?","choices":["It stops entirely, and the differentiated cells become immortal","It stays at the same relatively low rate as the stem cell","It becomes autonomous and no longer requires physiologic stimuli","It can be dramatic, but those cells have a limited life-span"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the stem cell itself proliferates slowly; the committed progeny do the expansion, and then die. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"By what mechanism does diabetes mellitus cause cataract?","choices":["Excess glucose glycates the lens capsule until it ruptures","Hyperglycemia causes crystallin proteins to aggregate directly","Retinal ischemia releases vascular endothelial growth factor into the lens","Excess glucose is converted to sorbitol, causing osmotic swelling of the lens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — in diabetes, excessive glucose is converted to sorbitol, and the resulting osmotic swelling of the lens produces cataract. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How differentiated are malignant tumors?","choices":["Always anaplastic","Always well differentiated","Various degrees of differentiation","Always poorly differentiated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — malignancy does not mean uniformly poor differentiation. Benign tumors are usually well differentiated. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which function of the eye does presbyopia impair?","choices":["Light detection by the rods and cones","Drainage of aqueous humor through the trabecular meshwork","Secretion of the lipid layer of the tear film","Accommodation for near objects"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — presbyopia is loss of lens elasticity and ciliary muscle accommodation due to sclerosis, so the eye can no longer focus on near objects. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which definitions of acute and chronic rhinosinusitis are accurate?","choices":["Acute under 2 weeks; chronic beyond 6 weeks, often with septal deviation","Acute under 4 weeks; chronic beyond 12 weeks despite therapy, often with nasal polyps","Acute under 12 weeks; chronic beyond 6 months, often with turbinate hypertrophy","Acute under 4 weeks; chronic beyond 12 weeks, usually with no mucosal change"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The two thresholds are the definition. Acute disease is usually viral &mdash; rhinovirus or influenza &mdash; with secondary bacterial infection by Streptococcus pneumoniae or Haemophilus influenzae. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which movement does the term exudation describe?","choices":["Fibrin into the vascular lumen to form a thrombus","Leukocytes from the interstitium back into the capillary lumen","Erythrocytes into the alveolar space","Fluid and blood proteins into the interstitial spaces"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exudation follows the increased capillary permeability produced by endothelial contraction. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which organisms most commonly cause bacterial otitis media?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus","Aspergillus niger and Candida albicans","Group A Streptococcus alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The classic middle ear trio. Viral causes are respiratory syncytial virus, rhinovirus, influenza and adenovirus. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How do cisplatin and carboplatin cause permanent bilateral sensorineural loss?","choices":["They cross-link DNA in stria vascularis cells, compromising endolymph ion homeostasis","They fix the stapes footplate in the oval window","They generate reactive oxygen species that destroy outer hair cells at the cochlear base","They inhibit prestin in outer hair cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The stria vascularis maintains the ionic composition of endolymph, and without that gradient transduction fails. Damage there is permanent and bilateral. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A patient has thick, leathery plaques on the neck and forearms with no scaling, after months of scratching. Which secondary lesion is this, mechanistically?","choices":["Ulcer, arising from focal loss of both the epidermis and the dermis beneath it","Atrophy, from slowed keratinocyte division","Lichenification, from itch-scratch-driven hyperplasia and hyperkeratosis","Scale, from desquamating stratum corneum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The absence of scaling is what separates it from psoriasis, whose plaques do scale. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which cells are the agranulocytes (mononuclear leukocytes)?","choices":["Basophils, mast cells and macrophages","Lymphocytes (B cells and T cells), monocytes and macrophages","Neutrophils, eosinophils and basophils","Erythrocytes, platelets and circulating plasma cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These three are grouped as the agranulocytes or mononuclear leukocytes. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is known about the pathogenesis of macular degeneration overall?","choices":["It is a consequence of raised intraocular pressure","It is unknown","It is entirely genetic","It is entirely driven by ultraviolet exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the pathogenesis of both dry and wet macular degeneration is unknown, even though drusen and choroidal neovascularization describe what happens in each. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Why does a deviated septum cause epistaxis and loss of smell?","choices":["Compression of the ethmoid arteries and the olfactory nerve","Turbulent airflow dries the mucosa; air misses the cribriform plate","Chronic infection eroding the septal vessels and olfactory epithelium","Autoimmune destruction of the septal cartilage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two separate consequences of the same disturbed airflow: local drying with fragile vessel breakdown on one hand, and failure to deliver odorant to the superior nasal vault on the other. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What happens to the wider nasal cavity opposite a septal deviation?","choices":["It atrophies from disuse","It develops polyps from the increased flow","Nothing changes; only the narrow side is affected","Compensatory inferior turbinate hypertrophy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The contralateral wide cavity undergoes compensatory inferior turbinate enlargement to humidify the increased volume of air passing through it. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Middle ear effusion and cholesteatoma impair conduction by which mechanism?","choices":["Mass loading","Stiffness effect","Discontinuity","Obstruction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both add fluid or tissue weight that damps movement of the tympanic membrane and ossicles. The chain is intact and mobile in principle, but it is carrying extra mass. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How does the mechanism of a hypopigmented macule such as vitiligo differ from that of a hyperpigmented macule?","choices":["Loss of melanocytes, against increased melanin production in the basal layer of the epidermis","Increased melanin production within the epidermis, as against loss of melanocytes entirely","Capillary rupture with blood deposition in the dermis","Dilatation and thinning of superficial dermal vessels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vitiligo reflects loss of melanocytes, while hyperpigmented macules such as ephelides, café au lait spots and Mongolian spots reflect increased basal melanin. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which human papillomavirus subtypes most commonly cause cervical cancer?","choices":["Types 1 and 2","Types 31 and 33","Types 16 and 18","Types 6 and 11"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — of many genetic subtypes, these two cause the majority. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which combination of cause and relationship to other exudates characterizes hemorrhagic inflammation?","choices":["Epithelial sloughing, and replacing other exudates","Damage to blood vessels, and occurring together with other exudates","Mucus hypersecretion, and occurring in isolation","Fibrinogen deposition, and preceding every other exudate pattern"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hemorrhagic inflammation is damage to blood vessels occurring with other exudates. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which structure separates the external ear from the middle ear?","choices":["The oval window, which passes energy to the cochlea","The round window, which relieves pressure","The Eustachian tube, which equalizes pressure","The tympanic membrane, which vibrates the ossicles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is the boundary and the first moving part of the conducting chain, which is why perforating it causes conductive loss. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"In the sequence of events of acute inflammation, which event immediately follows increased vascular permeability?","choices":["Hemoconcentration and stasis","Chemotaxis","Phagocytosis","Vasoconstriction of the arterioles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hemoconcentration and stasis come directly after increased vascular permeability and directly before leukocyte adhesion. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why are genes promoting self destruction of cells with damaged deoxyribonucleic acid necessary?","choices":["To limit the total number of cells an organ can contain","To supply nutrients to neighboring cells as they proliferate","To slow the rate at which stem cells commit to differentiation","To stop the damage becoming permanent in dividing cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — apoptosis prevents a mutation being carried on into the daughter cells. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What are the growth phases of melanoma, in order?","choices":["Sensitization then elicitation","Radial growth in the epidermis, vertical growth into the dermis, thickening, then lymphatic and vascular invasion","Vertical growth down into the dermis first, then radial spread within the epidermis afterwards once the tumor has thickened sufficiently to invade","Inflammatory, proliferative, then remodeling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Melanoma cells first multiply within the epidermis, then penetrate the dermis, grow deeper and thicker, and finally invade lymphatics and blood vessels. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes the primary component of the dermis?","choices":["Melanin, produced by melanocytes, providing ultraviolet protection","Collagen, produced by fibroblasts, providing tensile strength","Keratin, produced by keratinocytes, providing an impermeable barrier","Elastin, produced by mast cells, providing tensile strength"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Collagen is the primary component of the dermis; fibroblasts produce it, and it provides the skin's tensile strength. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which two structures make up the fibrous outer tunic of the eye?","choices":["The sclera and the cornea","The choroid and the ciliary body","The retina and the retinal pigment epithelium","The iris and the lens"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the protective white coat and the clear refracting window. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Cerumen impaction, foreign bodies and canal exostoses all cause conductive loss by which of the four core mechanisms?","choices":["Obstruction","Mass loading","Stiffness effect","Discontinuity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A physical blockage stops sound penetrating down the canal. It is the first of four mechanisms, and the only one that acts before the sound reaches the drum. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the classical corneal finding in herpes simplex epithelial keratitis?","choices":["A geographic ulcer with a hypopyon","A ring infiltrate in a contact lens wearer","A wedge of fibrovascular tissue growing across and over the corneal limbus","A dendritic ulcer with terminal bulbs on fluorescein staining"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the classical herpes simplex epithelial keratitis lesion is a branching dendritic corneal ulcer whose tips end in terminal bulbs on fluorescein stain. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A nodule is greater than 5 millimeters. What tissue does it arise from?","choices":["Fluid accumulating between epidermis and dermis","Dilated superficial dermal capillaries with no inflammatory infiltration at all","Dermal-based tissue with dense collagen bundles, extending into subcutaneous tissue","Superficial epidermis only, with no dermal involvement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A nodule is an elevated, solid lesion over 5 millimeters: a dermal-based lesion of dense collagen bundles extending into subcutaneous tissue. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which findings does orbital cellulitis show that periorbital cellulitis lacks?","choices":["Erythema and edema of the lid alone, held forward by the orbital septum","Crusting of the lid margin with altered tear film","A yellowish nodule that does not cross the cornea","Proptosis, reduced eye movement and risk of optic nerve compression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — bacteria breach the orbital septum into the soft tissue behind the globe, and the purulence there causes proptosis, reduced extraocular movement and optic nerve compression risk. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of primary open-angle glaucoma?","choices":["An open angle, but trabecular meshwork resistance impairs outflow","The iridocorneal angle narrows anatomically and blocks aqueous outflow suddenly","Aqueous production by the ciliary body increases sharply","The canal of Schlemm is congenitally absent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the angle is open; the obstruction is microscopic resistance in the trabecular meshwork, downstream, so pressure rises gradually. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of urticaria?","choices":["Capillary rupture with extravasation of blood","Mast cell degranulation releasing histamine, producing wheals","T cell-mediated delayed hypersensitivity 48 to 72 hours after exposure","Dysplastic keratinocyte proliferation from ultraviolet damage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In urticaria, mast cells release histamine, which causes vasodilation and plasma leakage into the dermis, forming wheals. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which description of the origin of melanoma is accurate?","choices":["At the dermal-epidermal junction, with ultraviolet light and oxidative stress damaging melanocyte DNA and leading to uninhibited growth","Within the stratum corneum, transformed by keratinase digestion","In the reticular layer of the dermis, transformed by dysregulation of the fibroblasts that reside within it over a long period of time","In the basal layer, transformed by Hedgehog pathway activation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Melanoma arises from melanocytes, found at the dermal-epidermal junction, when ultraviolet light and oxidative stress damage their DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid). — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What is the tissue distinction between an erosion and an ulcer?","choices":["An erosion stays above the dermal-epidermal junction; an ulcer loses epidermis AND dermis, destroying collagen with inflammatory infiltrate","An erosion involves both the epidermis and the dermis beneath it; an ulcer is limited to the epidermis alone and spares the dermal-epidermal junction entirely, so it never destroys collagen at all","An erosion is fluid-filled and an ulcer is solid","An erosion is a primary lesion and an ulcer is a secondary lesion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The junction is the boundary that separates them, and crossing it is what brings collagen destruction into play. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Why does labyrinthitis impair hearing AND balance together?","choices":["Dislodged otoconia striking structures in the endolymph","Fixation of the stapes footplate","Inflammation across both the semicircular canals and the cochlea","Defective endolymph resorption ballooning the scala media"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inflammatory swelling, vascular congestion and endolymphatic disruption affect the semicircular canals and the cochlea at once, impairing balance and the organ of Corti together. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What determines where a cancer metastasizes?","choices":["Venous blood flow, tumor and endothelial receptors, and genetic fitness","Chance alone, since circulating tumor cells lodge wherever the vessel first narrows","The size of the primary lesion and the duration of the disease","The histological grade of the tumor and its rate of mitosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — metastatic spread is NOT random but determined by these three. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Why does a tumor mass grow faster than normal tissue?","choices":["A lower proliferative fraction together with a lower rate of cell loss","An unchanged proliferative fraction with a shorter individual cell cycle","A higher proliferative fraction together with a lower rate of cell loss","A higher proliferative fraction together with a higher rate of cell loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both halves matter; it is not only that cells divide faster. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which four histological features move a tumor from the benign end towards the malignant end?","choices":["Encapsulation, uniform nuclei, absent mitoses, and normal differentiation","Invasion, lymphatic spread, hematogenous spread, and seeding","Initiation, promotion, progression, and metastasis","Pleomorphism, abnormal nuclei, mitoses, and abnormal differentiation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — pleomorphism, abnormal nuclei, mitoses and abnormal differentiation increase from the benign end toward the malignant end. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What causes astigmatism?","choices":["An axial globe that is too short","Loss of lens elasticity with age, so the lens can no longer change shape","Irregular corneal or lens curvature creating non-spherical focal points","An axial globe that is too long"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — irregular curvature of the cornea or lens creates non-spherical focal points instead of the single focal point needed for a sharp image. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which two mediators are plasma proteins?","choices":["Immunoglobulin G and C3b","Fibrinogen and fibrolytic enzymes","Bradykinin and complement components","Histamine and serotonin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bradykinin and complement components are both plasma proteins, as is the coagulation system. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which two cell types both act against virus-infected and tumor cells?","choices":["B lymphocytes and plasma cells","CD8+ cytotoxic T cells and natural killer cells","Monocytes and macrophages","T helper cells and regulatory suppressor T cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. CD8+ cytotoxic T cells and natural killer cells are the two subsets that act against virus-infected and tumor cells. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which description fits vocal cord nodules?","choices":["Unilateral pedunculated fluid-filled growths on the middle third, from a single severe strain","Bilateral wart-like exophytic lesions from viral infection","Unilateral firm masses with cord fixation, from malignancy","Bilateral symmetrical calluses at the anterior third junction, from chronic phonotrauma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic phonotrauma, the cords slamming together, causes basement membrane hyalinization, a callus. Nodules are bilateral and symmetrical at the anterior third junction. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which forms of radiation cause cancer?","choices":["Ultraviolet B, and ionizing radiation","Ultraviolet A only, together with infrared radiation","Microwave and radiofrequency radiation","Ionizing radiation only, with no ultraviolet contribution"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both ultraviolet B and ionizing radiation cause cancer; unrepaired ultraviolet B mutations explain the skin cancers of xeroderma pigmentosum. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which time course fits the vertigo of labyrinthitis?","choices":["Episodic, with attacks lasting several hours","Episodic, with attacks lasting under a minute","Continuous, permanent and non-improving","Sudden, lasting days, improving over weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Labyrinthitis vertigo is sudden and lasts days, improving over weeks, unlike the hours-long attacks of Meniere disease or the sub-minute attacks of positional vertigo. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which leukocyte is the first responder to microbial infection?","choices":["The eosinophil","The B lymphocyte","The neutrophil","The macrophage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The neutrophil is the first responder to microbial infection, defending against bacterial and fungal infection. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which three cell types are granulocytes?","choices":["Lymphocytes, monocytes and macrophages","Neutrophils, basophils and eosinophils","Neutrophils, monocytes and B lymphocytes","Basophils, eosinophils and natural killer cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the three granulocytes; polymorphonuclear leukocyte refers to all granulocytes. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which description of the proliferative phase of wound healing is accurate?","choices":["A fibrin hemostatic plug, over days 1 to 3","Type I collagen laid down in parallel bundles, over days 21 to one year of remodeling","Keratohyalin granules within the granular layer","Granulation tissue of macrophages, fibroblasts and endothelial cells, days 4 to 21"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Over days 4 to 21, granulation tissue forms, comprised of macrophages, fibroblasts and endothelial cells, before remodeling begins. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What are the symptoms of rhegmatogenous detachment?","choices":["Gradual painless bilateral blurring with glare at night","Severe pain with halos and a fixed mid-dilated pupil","Slow loss of central detail with progressive distortion of straight lines","Flashing lights, a shower of floaters, and a curtain falling across the field"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the symptoms of a full-thickness retinal break with detachment are flashing lights (photopsia), a shower of floaters and a curtain falling across the field. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Why does skeletal muscle hypertrophy rather than divide?","choices":["Protein intake causes fluid retention within the muscle, so the organ appears larger","Resistance training converts slow fibers to fast fibers, changing the tissue type","Striated muscle cells do not divide, so each fiber gets larger instead","Resistance training recruits satellite cells, so the number of muscle fibers increases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — skeletal muscle is a permanent tissue, so it grows by enlarging each existing fiber, which is hypertrophy rather than hyperplasia. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What happens when a protooncogene mutates?","choices":["It becomes a repair gene, which then introduces errors into the genome","It becomes silent, so the cell loses its ability to grow at all","It becomes an oncogene, which encodes oncoproteins that promote continued uncontrolled growth","It becomes a tumor suppressor gene, which then inhibits cell growth excessively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — mutation converts a protooncogene to an oncogene, whose oncoproteins promote continued growth in an uncontrolled manner. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which two abnormalities separate a tumor cell from a normal cell?","choices":["Abnormal apoptosis, and abnormal deoxyribonucleic acid repair","Abnormal vascular supply, and abnormal stromal composition","Abnormal differentiation, and abnormal cell size","Abnormal regulation of cell growth, and abnormal cell-cell interactions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — compared with normal cells, tumor cells show abnormal regulation of cell growth and abnormal cell-cell interactions. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What causes an ipsilateral optic nerve lesion?","choices":["Posterior cerebral artery occlusion in the occipital lobe","Temporal lobe surgery","Trauma, optic neuritis and ischemic optic neuropathy","Pituitary adenoma compression"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a lesion of the optic nerve before the chiasm follows trauma, optic neuritis or ischemic optic neuropathy, and causes total vision loss in that eye. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which pairing of maturation site and surface structure is correct for T lymphocytes?","choices":["The thymus; T cell receptors","The bone marrow; Fc receptors","The spleen; complement receptors","The lymph nodes; major histocompatibility complexes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. T lymphocytes mature in the thymus, which gives them their name, and carry T cell receptors on their surface. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 22"}]};
